  "question": {
    "text": "If $P$ and $Q$ are the points of intersection of the circles $x^2+y^2+3 x+7 y+2 p-5=0$ and $x^2+y^2+2 x+2 y-p^2=0$, then there is a circle passing through $P, Q$ and $(1,1)$ for",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "HRV2u",
      "text": "\nall values of $p$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "DqWXg",
      "text": "\nall except one value of $p$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "3gUPx",
      "text": "\nall except two values of $p$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "TVJnY",
      "text": "\nexactly one value of $p$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given circles $S=x^2+y^2+3 x+7 y+2 p-5=0$\n$$\nS^{\\prime}=x^2+y^2+2 x+2 y-p^2=0\n$$\nEquation of required circle is $S+\\lambda S^{\\prime}=0$\nAs it passes through $(1,1)$ the value of $\\lambda=\\frac{-(7+2 p)}{\\left(6-p^2\\right)}$\nIf $7+2 p=0$, it becomes the second circle\n$\\therefore$ it is true for all values of $p$",
    "image": null
  },
